1. Jenga

Jenga is still a very good choice for your architecture.

Its documentation says Jenga provides access to banks and mobile wallets across East and Central Africa, but service-level support differs. In particular, Jenga explicitly documents its Account Services as currently supporting Equity Bank. 

For Kenya, Jenga's payment gateway supports:

M-Pesa

Equitel

Equity bank payments


The bank-account payment option is currently documented as Equity Bank only. 

So for your Chama:

NUCSWG
   │
   ├── Equity Bank
   │       │
   │       └── Jenga
   │             ├── M-Pesa STK
   │             ├── Account balance
   │             ├── Statements
   │             └── Account inquiry
   │
   └── Members

That is a very clean V1-V5 architecture.


---

2. KCB BUNI

This is particularly interesting for your KCB Chama.

KCB's BUNI platform explicitly says its APIs connect to multiple payment networks including all banks and mobile money networks, including M-Pesa, Airtel Money, T-Kash and VOOMA. 

BUNI also provides:

Send Money

Receive Payments

Account Services

Interoperability APIs




However, there's an important condition:

> For production, the business/customer consuming BUNI APIs needs a KCB or VOOMA account. 



So:

KCB Chama
    ↓
KCB Bank Account
    ↓
KCB BUNI API
    ↓
Your FastAPI

This is potentially better than trying to force Jenga to handle a KCB account.


---

3. NCBA

NCBA is actually very interesting for your Chama idea because they explicitly provide API-based payment notifications and reconciliation.

Their corporate API solution provides automatic payment notifications to your system for auto-reconciliation. They also offer virtual accounts that can identify and attribute incoming collections from:

M-Pesa

EFT

PesaLink

SWIFT/RTGS




That maps extremely well to your original question:

> "If money enters the bank, how does my Chama system know?"



With a properly onboarded NCBA collection/reconciliation solution:

Member
   ↓
Payment
   ↓
NCBA
   ↓
Payment notification
   ↓
FastAPI
   ↓
Match transaction
   ↓
Contribution
   ↓
Ledger

That's exactly the kind of architecture you were looking for.


---

But there is a better architecture for your project

I wouldn't build the Chama system around one bank.

Instead:

┌── Jenga ── Equity
                    │
                    ├── KCB BUNI ── KCB
                    │
Chama FastAPI ──────┼── NCBA API ── NCBA
                    │
                    ├── Daraja ── M-Pesa
                    │
                    └── Future Provider

Your application should have a payment/banking provider layer.

For example:

PaymentProvider
       │
       ├── JengaProvider
       │
       ├── KCBProvider
       │
       ├── NCBAProvider
       │
       └── DarajaProvider

Then your Chama business logic doesn't care whether the group uses Equity, KCB or NCBA.


